aid agencies say an estimated 40 000  children are trapped in the el hall and  raj refugee camps in northeast syria  every day is a struggle for survival and  the statistics tell a grim story save  the children says at least two have died  every week since the start of the year  some were killed from avoidable  illnesses others by fires poor  sanitation malnutrition and even murder  living conditions are tough and daily  outbreaks of violence are making them  worse  they're witnessing so much  trauma around them they're living  through it every day  and we're really concerned about what  this means for children growing up in  these conditions it's simply no place  for a child to grow up and so we're  calling strongly on governments to  repatriate their children bring them  back to their countries and give them a  chance for a proper life  calls to repatriate the foreign  nationals among the camp's 60 000  residents are not new at least 30 000  are from iraq and 10 000 are from other  countries many are families of isil  fighters or those who lived under their  control before the armed group was  defeated in the region three years ago  some countries have taken back their  citizens but many have not  since 2017 nearly 1200 children have  been repatriated almost 59 percent of  them went home in 2019  but repatriations declined in 2020 and  so far this year there have been only  14.  the united nations is calling for action  from the nearly 60 governments who are  believed to have nationals in the camps  but with differences on policy possible  security threats and the lack of  political will many are reluctant each  of those countries have their own local  situation with  their own complexities in terms of the  political environment so this is  something that each government is having  to to work through as they consider the  repetition of their nationals the second  level i would say is the legal one and  so in many countries as well there are  complexities in terms of the actual  nationality of these children  and mothers  eighty percent of the camp's residents  are women and children sixty-five  percent of them are under the age of 18.  they're vulnerable not just to the dire  conditions but also to the threat of  radicalization in camps described as the  most dangerous in the world and with the  slow pace of repatriations it will take  decades before they can leave
 

